# API Design Patterns

## Route Structure

API routes follow Next.js App Router conventions under `apps/api/src/app/api/`:

```
api/
├── webhooks/     # Inbound webhooks (Clerk, Stripe) - NO auth required
├── users/        # User profile management
├── professionals/ # Professional listing, registration
├── consultations/ # Booking, history, video join
├── prescriptions/ # Create, list prescriptions
├── payments/     # Stripe checkout, subscriptions
└── health/       # Health check endpoint
```

## Validation

- **Always validate** request bodies using Zod schemas from `@telemed/shared/validators`
- Parse and handle validation errors with `handleApiError()` in `lib/api-utils.ts`
- Validation schemas are centralized in `packages/shared/src/validators/`

## Response Format

All API responses use a consistent wrapper:

```typescript
// Success
{ data: T, error: null }

// Error
{ data: null, error: { code: string, message: string, details?: unknown } }
```

Use helpers: `apiResponse()`, `apiError()`, `apiPaginatedResponse()`, `handleApiError()`

## Authentication

- All API routes (except webhooks and health) require Clerk authentication
- Middleware enforces auth at the route level
- Use `auth()` from `@clerk/nextjs/server` to get the current user
- Verify ownership for mutations (users can only modify their own data)

## Error Handling

- Catch all errors and return structured responses
- Never expose internal error details to clients
- Log errors to console (Sentry captures them automatically)
- Use specific HTTP status codes (400, 401, 403, 404, 500)
